{
  "dom_id": "step-q159508",
  "name": "Question 13",
  "problem": "Previous studies show that the scores of girls and boys on a particular test for dyslexia have standard deviations of $2$ points and $4$ points, respectively. Scientists want to determine whether the population mean ${\\mu }_{x}$ for girls is the same as the population mean ${\\mu }_{y}$ for boys. They construct a $99%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means.\n\nScientists sampled $125$ girls and $118$ boys and found that their mean scores on the test were $42$ points and $41$ points. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The end-points of the $99%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means are approximately $1\\pm 1.055.$\n\nII. All values contained within the $99%$ confidence interval are positive.\n\nIII. The confidence interval data suggests that ${\\mu }_{x}>{\\mu }_{y}.$\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |",
  "worked_solution": "",
